1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,175
Look at almost any modern wind turbine

2
00:00:03,175 --> 00:00:05,766
on Earth and you will count the

3
00:00:05,766 --> 00:00:07,604
same number of blades.

4
00:00:07,784 --> 00:00:08,306
Three.

5
00:00:08,486 --> 00:00:10,193
Not two, not four,

6
00:00:10,193 --> 00:00:13,699
not twenty like an old farm windmill.

7
00:00:13,879 --> 00:00:14,530
Three.

8
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:17,773
Engineers tried everything else,

9
00:00:17,773 --> 00:00:19,878
one blade, two blades,

10
00:00:19,878 --> 00:00:23,229
many blades, and almost all of them

11
00:00:23,229 --> 00:00:23,707
lost.

12
00:00:23,887 --> 00:00:27,248
Today, eleven parts of a wind turbine,

13
00:00:27,248 --> 00:00:29,370
and why each one exists,

14
00:00:29,370 --> 00:00:32,200
starting with that magic number.

15
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:35,416
Modern wind turbines are some of the

16
00:00:35,416 --> 00:00:37,646
biggest machines ever built.

17
00:00:37,826 --> 00:00:40,209
The tallest ones reach more than two

18
00:00:40,209 --> 00:00:42,328
hundred meters to the tip of the

19
00:00:42,328 --> 00:00:45,373
blade, taller than most skyscrapers in Europe.

20
00:00:45,553 --> 00:00:48,210
A single blade can be longer than

21
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:49,579
a football field,

22
00:00:49,579 --> 00:00:52,639
and one turbine can power thousands of

23
00:00:52,639 --> 00:00:53,122
homes.

24
00:00:53,473 --> 00:00:56,384
As always, every fact comes from a

25
00:00:56,384 --> 00:00:58,867
named source, turbine makers,

26
00:00:58,867 --> 00:01:02,978
energy laboratories, and engineering references,

27
00:01:02,978 --> 00:01:05,547
all listed in the description.

28
00:01:05,727 --> 00:01:07,737
Let's start with the question in the

29
00:01:07,737 --> 00:01:08,072
title.

30
00:01:08,423 --> 00:01:11,688
Why do wind turbines have three blades,

31
00:01:11,688 --> 00:01:12,776
and not more?

32
00:01:12,956 --> 00:01:15,972
Because more blades do not catch much

33
00:01:15,972 --> 00:01:16,950
more energy.

34
00:01:17,130 --> 00:01:19,439
A rotor can never take all the

35
00:01:19,439 --> 00:01:21,055
energy from the wind,

36
00:01:21,055 --> 00:01:23,826
there is a physical limit called the

37
00:01:23,826 --> 00:01:26,674
Betz limit, about fifty nine percent.

38
00:01:26,854 --> 00:01:30,041
Three slim blades spinning fast already get

39
00:01:30,041 --> 00:01:32,043
close to what is practical.

40
00:01:32,223 --> 00:01:34,641
A fourth blade would add a lot

41
00:01:34,641 --> 00:01:36,978
of cost and weight for only a

42
00:01:36,978 --> 00:01:37,784
tiny gain.

43
00:01:38,135 --> 00:01:39,131
So why not two,

44
00:01:39,131 --> 00:01:40,991
which would be even cheaper?

45
00:01:41,171 --> 00:01:43,214
Because two blades wobble.

46
00:01:43,394 --> 00:01:46,417
When the turbine turns to face the

47
00:01:46,417 --> 00:01:49,884
wind, a two bladed rotor is unbalanced.

48
00:01:50,064 --> 00:01:52,095
When its blades are vertical,

49
00:01:52,095 --> 00:01:53,426
it resists turning,

50
00:01:53,426 --> 00:01:55,458
and when they are horizontal,

51
00:01:55,458 --> 00:01:56,299
it does not.

52
00:01:56,479 --> 00:01:58,829
That flips twice every rotation,

53
00:01:58,829 --> 00:02:00,739
shaking the whole machine.

54
00:02:00,919 --> 00:02:02,379
With three blades,

55
00:02:02,379 --> 00:02:05,703
the forces stay smooth in every position.

56
00:02:06,053 --> 00:02:08,892
Two bladed turbines also have to spin

57
00:02:08,892 --> 00:02:12,116
faster, which makes them louder and harder

58
00:02:12,116 --> 00:02:12,960
on the eye.

59
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:15,379
Three blades turn at a calm,

60
00:02:15,379 --> 00:02:18,417
steady pace that people find easier to

61
00:02:18,417 --> 00:02:19,456
live next to.

62
00:02:19,636 --> 00:02:23,575
Some designers still build two bladed turbines

63
00:02:23,575 --> 00:02:25,116
for special cases,

64
00:02:25,116 --> 00:02:27,942
like floating offshore platforms,

65
00:02:27,942 --> 00:02:28,969
but on land,

66
00:02:28,969 --> 00:02:30,596
three won the race.

67
00:02:30,946 --> 00:02:32,944
Why is the end of a wind

68
00:02:32,944 --> 00:02:35,775
turbine blade covered in a ring of

69
00:02:35,775 --> 00:02:36,774
steel bolts?

70
00:02:36,954 --> 00:02:39,376
Because this is where the whole blade

71
00:02:39,376 --> 00:02:40,750
hangs on the turbine.

72
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:43,975
Every rotation, the blade is pulled by

73
00:02:43,975 --> 00:02:45,257
gravity one way,

74
00:02:45,257 --> 00:02:46,460
then the other,

75
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:48,303
and pushed by the wind.

76
00:02:48,483 --> 00:02:51,224
All of that force passes through this

77
00:02:51,224 --> 00:02:51,594
ring.

78
00:02:51,774 --> 00:02:54,668
Large blades are fixed with around a

79
00:02:54,668 --> 00:02:56,436
hundred or more bolts,

80
00:02:56,436 --> 00:02:59,170
each tightened to a precise force.

81
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,898
A blade turns maybe fifteen times a

82
00:03:02,898 --> 00:03:07,050
minute, which means millions of load cycles

83
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:08,401
over its life.

84
00:03:08,581 --> 00:03:11,634
Bolts that are loose or tightened unevenly

85
00:03:11,634 --> 00:03:13,306
can crack from fatigue.

86
00:03:13,486 --> 00:03:17,281
So technicians check and re-tighten them during

87
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:20,510
maintenance, often with hydraulic tools,

88
00:03:20,510 --> 00:03:23,498
because a human arm cannot reach that

89
00:03:23,498 --> 00:03:23,982
force.

90
00:03:24,332 --> 00:03:26,679
When a blade does break off,

91
00:03:26,679 --> 00:03:30,032
the result is spectacular and dangerous.

92
00:03:30,212 --> 00:03:33,045
Pieces can be thrown hundreds of meters.

93
00:03:33,225 --> 00:03:35,594
That is why turbines are built away

94
00:03:35,594 --> 00:03:37,083
from houses and roads,

95
00:03:37,083 --> 00:03:39,114
and why the root of the blade,

96
00:03:39,114 --> 00:03:40,468
where the bolts sit,

97
00:03:40,468 --> 00:03:42,972
is one of the most carefully designed

98
00:03:42,972 --> 00:03:44,800
parts of the whole machine.

99
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:48,172
Why can each wind turbine blade rotate

100
00:03:48,172 --> 00:03:49,921
around its own length?

101
00:03:50,101 --> 00:03:53,213
Because that is how the turbine controls

102
00:03:53,213 --> 00:03:53,991
its power.

103
00:03:54,171 --> 00:03:55,539
Inside the hub,

104
00:03:55,539 --> 00:03:59,736
motors or hydraulic cylinders turn every blade

105
00:03:59,736 --> 00:04:01,926
a few degrees at a time.

106
00:04:02,106 --> 00:04:03,964
This is called pitch.

107
00:04:04,144 --> 00:04:05,126
In light wind,

108
00:04:05,126 --> 00:04:07,442
the blades face the wind to catch

109
00:04:07,442 --> 00:04:08,846
as much as possible.

110
00:04:09,026 --> 00:04:10,349
In strong wind,

111
00:04:10,349 --> 00:04:13,261
they turn away to spill the extra

112
00:04:13,261 --> 00:04:13,879
energy.

113
00:04:14,229 --> 00:04:15,041
In a storm,

114
00:04:15,041 --> 00:04:17,921
the blades turn almost fully edge-on to

115
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:19,767
the wind, like a feather.

116
00:04:19,947 --> 00:04:21,908
They stop catching wind,

117
00:04:21,908 --> 00:04:23,952
and the rotor slows down.

118
00:04:24,132 --> 00:04:26,035
That is the main brake of a

119
00:04:26,035 --> 00:04:27,093
modern turbine.

120
00:04:27,273 --> 00:04:30,008
Most turbines shut down this way when

121
00:04:30,008 --> 00:04:32,817
the wind goes above around twenty five

122
00:04:32,817 --> 00:04:34,148
meters per second,

123
00:04:34,148 --> 00:04:36,514
about ninety kilometers an hour.

124
00:04:36,864 --> 00:04:39,781
Because this brake is so important,

125
00:04:39,781 --> 00:04:42,698
each blade has its own pitch system

126
00:04:42,698 --> 00:04:44,782
and its own backup power,

127
00:04:44,782 --> 00:04:47,115
batteries or pressure tanks.

128
00:04:47,295 --> 00:04:49,763
If the grid fails and the turbine

129
00:04:49,763 --> 00:04:52,754
loses power, the blades still feather by

130
00:04:52,754 --> 00:04:53,576
themselves.

131
00:04:53,756 --> 00:04:56,812
One working blade is enough to slow

132
00:04:56,812 --> 00:04:58,122
the rotor down.

133
00:04:58,472 --> 00:05:01,038
Why do some wind turbine blades have

134
00:05:01,038 --> 00:05:03,534
rows of small fins near their base?

135
00:05:03,714 --> 00:05:06,337
They are called vortex generators.

136
00:05:06,517 --> 00:05:09,066
Each fin creates a tiny swirl of

137
00:05:09,066 --> 00:05:12,013
air, which mixes fast moving air into

138
00:05:12,013 --> 00:05:14,561
the thin layer right next to the

139
00:05:14,561 --> 00:05:15,039
blade.

140
00:05:15,219 --> 00:05:17,685
That keeps the airflow stuck to the

141
00:05:17,685 --> 00:05:20,432
surface, instead of separating from it.

142
00:05:20,782 --> 00:05:21,795
Near the root,

143
00:05:21,795 --> 00:05:24,401
the blade is thick and turns slowly,

144
00:05:24,401 --> 00:05:26,718
and air tends to break away from

145
00:05:26,718 --> 00:05:26,935
it.

146
00:05:27,115 --> 00:05:29,121
When the flow separates,

147
00:05:29,121 --> 00:05:30,542
the blade stalls,

148
00:05:30,542 --> 00:05:33,217
like an airplane wing at a steep

149
00:05:33,217 --> 00:05:35,056
angle, and loses lift.

150
00:05:35,236 --> 00:05:37,553
The little fins delay that stall,

151
00:05:37,553 --> 00:05:39,660
so the inner part of the blade

152
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:41,064
produces more power.

153
00:05:41,415 --> 00:05:44,035
The same trick is used on airplane

154
00:05:44,035 --> 00:05:46,810
wings, where you can sometimes see a

155
00:05:46,810 --> 00:05:49,354
row of tiny tabs from your window

156
00:05:49,354 --> 00:05:49,739
seat.

157
00:05:49,919 --> 00:05:52,491
On turbines, the fins can even be

158
00:05:52,491 --> 00:05:55,298
glued onto older blades years later,

159
00:05:55,298 --> 00:05:57,792
and makers report gains of a few

160
00:05:57,792 --> 00:05:59,195
percent in energy.

161
00:05:59,375 --> 00:06:02,162
For a turbine running for twenty years,

162
00:06:02,162 --> 00:06:03,091
that adds up.

163
00:06:03,441 --> 00:06:06,057
Why do wind turbine blades have metal

164
00:06:06,057 --> 00:06:08,108
points built into their tips?

165
00:06:08,288 --> 00:06:10,956
Because a turbine is often the tallest

166
00:06:10,956 --> 00:06:12,431
thing for kilometers,

167
00:06:12,431 --> 00:06:14,046
and lightning loves it.

168
00:06:14,226 --> 00:06:17,419
The blade itself is made of fiberglass

169
00:06:17,419 --> 00:06:20,949
or carbon, which cannot carry the current.

170
00:06:21,129 --> 00:06:23,925
So metal receptors on the blade surface

171
00:06:23,925 --> 00:06:25,145
catch the strike,

172
00:06:25,145 --> 00:06:27,583
and a thick cable inside the blade

173
00:06:27,583 --> 00:06:29,519
carries it down to the hub,

174
00:06:29,519 --> 00:06:31,670
the tower and into the ground.

175
00:06:32,021 --> 00:06:33,238
Without that path,

176
00:06:33,238 --> 00:06:35,942
lightning would find its own way through

177
00:06:35,942 --> 00:06:36,618
the blade.

178
00:06:36,798 --> 00:06:39,821
The current heats the air and moisture

179
00:06:39,821 --> 00:06:42,446
inside to explosive temperatures,

180
00:06:42,446 --> 00:06:45,071
which can crack the blade open or

181
00:06:45,071 --> 00:06:46,423
blow off its tip.

182
00:06:46,603 --> 00:06:49,801
Lightning is one of the leading causes

183
00:06:49,801 --> 00:06:51,990
of blade damage worldwide.

184
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:55,270
Turbines in places with lots of storms

185
00:06:55,270 --> 00:06:57,506
can be hit many times a year.

186
00:06:57,686 --> 00:06:59,063
After a strike,

187
00:06:59,063 --> 00:07:01,082
sensors can record it,

188
00:07:01,082 --> 00:07:05,488
and drones or technicians inspect the receptors.

189
00:07:05,668 --> 00:07:08,756
A turbine is not just catching wind.

190
00:07:08,936 --> 00:07:11,703
It is also catching lightning,

191
00:07:11,703 --> 00:07:13,917
safely, again and again.

192
00:07:14,267 --> 00:07:16,975
Why do technicians hang from ropes on

193
00:07:16,975 --> 00:07:18,440
wind turbine blades,

194
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:20,490
a hundred meters in the air?

195
00:07:20,670 --> 00:07:22,854
Because the blades wear out,

196
00:07:22,854 --> 00:07:25,116
especially at the front edge.

197
00:07:25,296 --> 00:07:27,558
The tip of a big blade moves

198
00:07:27,558 --> 00:07:31,032
at around three hundred kilometers an hour.

199
00:07:31,212 --> 00:07:32,431
At that speed,

200
00:07:32,431 --> 00:07:36,004
raindrops, hail, sand and insects hit the

201
00:07:36,004 --> 00:07:39,227
edge like tiny bullets and slowly eat

202
00:07:39,227 --> 00:07:40,708
away the surface.

203
00:07:40,888 --> 00:07:43,443
This is called leading edge erosion.

204
00:07:43,793 --> 00:07:46,824
A rough edge disturbs the airflow and

205
00:07:46,824 --> 00:07:49,528
steals power, and if it gets deep

206
00:07:49,528 --> 00:07:52,314
enough, water can enter the blade.

207
00:07:52,494 --> 00:07:55,073
So rope access technicians,

208
00:07:55,073 --> 00:07:59,084
trained climbers, abseil down the blade to

209
00:07:59,084 --> 00:08:02,618
sand, fill and coat the damaged edge.

210
00:08:02,798 --> 00:08:05,651
Some blades now get protective tapes or

211
00:08:05,651 --> 00:08:07,553
shields on the front edge,

212
00:08:07,553 --> 00:08:09,602
like a stone guard on a car.

213
00:08:09,952 --> 00:08:13,151
The turbine is stopped and locked before

214
00:08:13,151 --> 00:08:14,990
anyone goes on a blade.

215
00:08:15,170 --> 00:08:17,667
Then the work is done from ropes

216
00:08:17,667 --> 00:08:20,631
or platforms hanging from the nacelle,

217
00:08:20,631 --> 00:08:22,113
often in cold wind.

218
00:08:22,293 --> 00:08:24,876
Drones now help find the damage first,

219
00:08:24,876 --> 00:08:27,188
so the climbers only go where they

220
00:08:27,188 --> 00:08:27,936
are needed.

221
00:08:28,286 --> 00:08:31,165
Why do wind turbines in cold places

222
00:08:31,165 --> 00:08:33,139
worry so much about ice?

223
00:08:33,319 --> 00:08:36,368
Because ice on a blade changes its

224
00:08:36,368 --> 00:08:36,906
shape.

225
00:08:37,086 --> 00:08:38,698
It ruins the airfoil,

226
00:08:38,698 --> 00:08:40,848
and the turbine loses power.

227
00:08:41,028 --> 00:08:43,533
Ice also adds weight unevenly,

228
00:08:43,533 --> 00:08:47,123
so the rotor becomes unbalanced and shakes.

229
00:08:47,303 --> 00:08:49,817
And when chunks break off a spinning

230
00:08:49,817 --> 00:08:52,051
blade, they can be thrown a long

231
00:08:52,051 --> 00:08:52,330
way.

232
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:56,247
So turbines in cold regions carry sensors

233
00:08:56,247 --> 00:08:57,814
that detect icing,

234
00:08:57,814 --> 00:09:01,556
and many stop automatically when ice builds

235
00:09:01,556 --> 00:09:01,817
up.

236
00:09:01,997 --> 00:09:04,954
Warning signs around them tell walkers to

237
00:09:04,954 --> 00:09:06,397
keep away in winter.

238
00:09:06,577 --> 00:09:09,559
In places like Scandinavia and Canada,

239
00:09:09,559 --> 00:09:13,011
some blades are fitted with heating systems,

240
00:09:13,011 --> 00:09:15,836
warm air blown through the inside or

241
00:09:15,836 --> 00:09:18,268
heating mats under the surface.

242
00:09:18,618 --> 00:09:20,991
Heating costs some energy,

243
00:09:20,991 --> 00:09:24,185
but a stopped turbine makes none at

244
00:09:24,185 --> 00:09:24,550
all.

245
00:09:24,730 --> 00:09:26,167
In cold climates,

246
00:09:26,167 --> 00:09:29,209
a turbine that can de-ice itself may

247
00:09:29,209 --> 00:09:32,335
produce far more power over a winter.

248
00:09:32,515 --> 00:09:35,306
It is the same problem airplanes face,

249
00:09:35,306 --> 00:09:38,023
solved on a machine that never leaves

250
00:09:38,023 --> 00:09:38,831
the ground.

251
00:09:39,181 --> 00:09:41,511
Why does a wind turbine have a

252
00:09:41,511 --> 00:09:43,375
box the size of a bus on

253
00:09:43,375 --> 00:09:44,695
top of its tower?

254
00:09:44,875 --> 00:09:47,163
Because that is the power plant.

255
00:09:47,343 --> 00:09:50,045
The nacelle holds the main shaft,

256
00:09:50,045 --> 00:09:52,338
the bearings, the generator,

257
00:09:52,338 --> 00:09:54,058
and on many turbines,

258
00:09:54,058 --> 00:09:54,877
a gearbox.

259
00:09:55,057 --> 00:09:57,011
The rotor turns slowly,

260
00:09:57,011 --> 00:10:00,070
maybe ten to fifteen times a minute,

261
00:10:00,070 --> 00:10:03,129
but a normal generator wants to spin

262
00:10:03,129 --> 00:10:04,148
much faster.

263
00:10:04,328 --> 00:10:06,937
The gearbox speeds it up about a

264
00:10:06,937 --> 00:10:08,078
hundred times.

265
00:10:08,428 --> 00:10:11,011
Gearboxes are one of the parts that

266
00:10:11,011 --> 00:10:12,191
fail most often,

267
00:10:12,191 --> 00:10:14,922
and replacing one at a hundred meters

268
00:10:14,922 --> 00:10:16,915
height needs a giant crane.

269
00:10:17,095 --> 00:10:19,115
So some makers removed it.

270
00:10:19,295 --> 00:10:22,756
Direct drive turbines use a very large

271
00:10:22,756 --> 00:10:25,762
generator, several meters across,

272
00:10:25,762 --> 00:10:28,768
that turns at the same slow speed

273
00:10:28,768 --> 00:10:29,953
as the rotor.

274
00:10:30,133 --> 00:10:31,730
Fewer moving parts,

275
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:35,345
but a heavier and more expensive generator.

276
00:10:35,696 --> 00:10:38,469
The nacelle also turns to face the

277
00:10:38,469 --> 00:10:38,877
wind.

278
00:10:39,057 --> 00:10:41,451
A wind vane and an anemometer on

279
00:10:41,451 --> 00:10:44,370
its roof measure the wind direction and

280
00:10:44,370 --> 00:10:47,214
speed, and yaw motors rotate the whole

281
00:10:47,214 --> 00:10:49,534
nacelle on a big bearing at the

282
00:10:49,534 --> 00:10:50,806
top of the tower.

283
00:10:50,986 --> 00:10:53,261
On the biggest offshore turbines,

284
00:10:53,261 --> 00:10:56,088
everything up there can weigh hundreds of

285
00:10:56,088 --> 00:10:56,570
tonnes.

286
00:10:56,921 --> 00:10:59,188
Why is there a ladder inside a

287
00:10:59,188 --> 00:11:00,624
wind turbine tower?

288
00:11:00,804 --> 00:11:03,677
Because that is how technicians get to

289
00:11:03,677 --> 00:11:04,055
work.

290
00:11:04,235 --> 00:11:06,948
The tower is a hollow steel tube,

291
00:11:06,948 --> 00:11:10,400
sometimes more than a hundred meters tall.

292
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:13,155
Inside, a ladder runs all the way

293
00:11:13,155 --> 00:11:15,886
up, with rest platforms on the way.

294
00:11:16,066 --> 00:11:18,919
Many modern turbines also have a small

295
00:11:18,919 --> 00:11:21,773
service lift, but the ladder is always

296
00:11:21,773 --> 00:11:23,124
there as a backup.

297
00:11:23,475 --> 00:11:26,391
Climbing it is like climbing a thirty

298
00:11:26,391 --> 00:11:27,573
story building.

299
00:11:27,753 --> 00:11:31,537
Technicians wear harnesses clipped to a safety

300
00:11:31,537 --> 00:11:34,087
rail, so a slip cannot become a

301
00:11:34,087 --> 00:11:34,498
fall.

302
00:11:34,678 --> 00:11:37,356
They carry tools and spare parts in

303
00:11:37,356 --> 00:11:39,574
bags, or use a small crane in

304
00:11:39,574 --> 00:11:42,405
the nacelle to lift heavy pieces from

305
00:11:42,405 --> 00:11:43,246
the ground.

306
00:11:43,597 --> 00:11:46,558
The tower also hides the power cables,

307
00:11:46,558 --> 00:11:49,909
the control cabinets and the transformer on

308
00:11:49,909 --> 00:11:50,922
some designs.

309
00:11:51,102 --> 00:11:52,101
It is tapered,

310
00:11:52,101 --> 00:11:54,741
wider at the bottom where the bending

311
00:11:54,741 --> 00:11:56,097
force is strongest,

312
00:11:56,097 --> 00:11:58,736
and built in sections that are bolted

313
00:11:58,736 --> 00:11:59,949
together on site.

314
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:03,179
Why does moving a single wind turbine

315
00:12:03,179 --> 00:12:05,280
blade need a police escort?

316
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:07,965
Because the blade is made in one

317
00:12:07,965 --> 00:12:10,705
piece, and it is longer than almost

318
00:12:10,705 --> 00:12:12,740
anything else on the road.

319
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:15,592
On land, blades of sixty to eighty

320
00:12:15,592 --> 00:12:17,006
meters are common.

321
00:12:17,186 --> 00:12:18,279
They cannot bend,

322
00:12:18,279 --> 00:12:20,142
and they cannot be cut in two

323
00:12:20,142 --> 00:12:21,877
without making them weaker.

324
00:12:22,057 --> 00:12:23,744
So they travel whole,

325
00:12:23,744 --> 00:12:26,155
on special extending trailers.

326
00:12:26,506 --> 00:12:29,338
Every route is planned in advance.

327
00:12:29,518 --> 00:12:33,126
Corners, bridges, trees and road signs are

328
00:12:33,126 --> 00:12:36,132
checked, and some are removed for a

329
00:12:36,132 --> 00:12:36,647
night.

330
00:12:36,827 --> 00:12:40,054
In mountains, special trailers can tilt the

331
00:12:40,054 --> 00:12:41,705
blade up into the air,

332
00:12:41,705 --> 00:12:44,106
so it can pass tight curves with

333
00:12:44,106 --> 00:12:46,208
its tip pointing at the sky.

334
00:12:46,558 --> 00:12:49,721
This limit on transport is one reason

335
00:12:49,721 --> 00:12:53,140
the biggest turbines are built offshore.

336
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:55,795
Blades of more than a hundred meters

337
00:12:55,795 --> 00:12:58,064
can be loaded onto ships right at

338
00:12:58,064 --> 00:12:59,782
the factory on the coast,

339
00:12:59,782 --> 00:13:02,394
and taken to sea without ever touching

340
00:13:02,394 --> 00:13:02,875
a road.

341
00:13:03,226 --> 00:13:06,674
Why do some offshore wind turbines stand

342
00:13:06,674 --> 00:13:09,433
on these steel towers with legs,

343
00:13:09,433 --> 00:13:10,726
called jackets?

344
00:13:10,906 --> 00:13:12,995
Because the sea gets deep.

345
00:13:13,175 --> 00:13:14,620
In shallow water,

346
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:17,935
a turbine usually stands on a monopile,

347
00:13:17,935 --> 00:13:21,250
a single giant steel tube hammered into

348
00:13:21,250 --> 00:13:22,185
the seabed.

349
00:13:22,365 --> 00:13:24,102
But the deeper the water,

350
00:13:24,102 --> 00:13:26,742
the thicker and heavier that tube must

351
00:13:26,742 --> 00:13:26,951
be.

352
00:13:27,131 --> 00:13:30,307
A lattice jacket with four legs spreads

353
00:13:30,307 --> 00:13:32,669
the load and uses less steel.

354
00:13:33,019 --> 00:13:35,574
The foundation must survive waves,

355
00:13:35,574 --> 00:13:38,655
currents, and the huge bending force from

356
00:13:38,655 --> 00:13:39,857
the rotor above.

357
00:13:40,037 --> 00:13:42,824
Engineers design it for the worst storm

358
00:13:42,824 --> 00:13:44,539
expected in fifty years,

359
00:13:44,539 --> 00:13:47,040
and for decades of waves hitting it

360
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:48,326
every few seconds.

361
00:13:48,506 --> 00:13:50,032
Below the waterline,

362
00:13:50,032 --> 00:13:53,388
it is protected from corrosion with coatings

363
00:13:53,388 --> 00:13:56,439
and metal anodes that corrode instead of

364
00:13:56,439 --> 00:13:57,202
the steel.

365
00:13:57,553 --> 00:14:00,051
Where the sea is too deep for

366
00:14:00,051 --> 00:14:01,861
any fixed foundation,

367
00:14:01,861 --> 00:14:03,843
turbines can now float,

368
00:14:03,843 --> 00:14:06,945
held in place by anchors and chains,

369
00:14:06,945 --> 00:14:08,582
like oil platforms.

370
00:14:08,762 --> 00:14:11,022
It is the same old problem,

371
00:14:11,022 --> 00:14:14,202
keeping a tall machine standing in the

372
00:14:14,202 --> 00:14:16,378
wind, solved one more way.

373
00:14:16,729 --> 00:14:18,517
So here are the eleven.

374
00:14:18,697 --> 00:14:22,332
Three blades, because two wobble and four

375
00:14:22,332 --> 00:14:23,573
cost too much.

376
00:14:23,753 --> 00:14:25,904
A ring of bolts at the root,

377
00:14:25,904 --> 00:14:28,133
a hub that twists the blades,

378
00:14:28,133 --> 00:14:30,668
little fins that delay the stall,

379
00:14:30,668 --> 00:14:33,819
and tips that catch lightning on purpose.

380
00:14:33,999 --> 00:14:36,724
Workers on ropes fixing the edges,

381
00:14:36,724 --> 00:14:39,049
ice that turns a blade into a

382
00:14:39,049 --> 00:14:41,454
danger, a machine room on top,

383
00:14:41,454 --> 00:14:42,736
a ladder inside,

384
00:14:42,736 --> 00:14:45,141
a blade too long for the road,

385
00:14:45,141 --> 00:14:47,466
and steel legs under the sea.

386
00:14:47,816 --> 00:14:50,022
Next time you see a wind farm

387
00:14:50,022 --> 00:14:51,924
turning slowly on a hill,

388
00:14:51,924 --> 00:14:53,218
count the blades,

389
00:14:53,218 --> 00:14:56,109
and remember that every number on that

390
00:14:56,109 --> 00:14:57,707
machine was a choice.

391
00:14:57,887 --> 00:15:00,918
If you want more machines explained part

392
00:15:00,918 --> 00:15:03,797
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

393
00:15:03,977 --> 00:15:05,936
And tell us in the comments which

394
00:15:05,936 --> 00:15:07,894
machine you want us to take apart

395
00:15:07,894 --> 00:15:08,191
next.
